{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md", "content": "# Rejected global-fill hypothesis: preserve geometry, audit exact changes

In this Continual-ARC instance, several demos used noisy grids with one distinctive-color object. An attempted submission globally flood-filled every zero connected to the marker color, and binary feedback rejected it. The failure illustrates why connectivity alone is not sufficient evidence for recoloring: the demos preserve many background cells, and different examples show selective additions localized to patterned regions rather than blanket access from a seed.

After rejection, do not make a small arbitrary edit or repeat the same flood-fill with a different boundary. Reconstruct exact changed coordinates for every input/output pair, identify the marker object's shape and candidate repeated/translated structures, and check every unchanged background region. If the demos still support incompatible rules, request another only with a stated discriminating question; otherwise use a materially different candidate and validate it against the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md", "content": "# Unchanged demos and hypothesis revision

An input-output pair with no changes is an active constraint, not a non-example. A salient sparse motif may be inert or lack the feature that triggers the demonstrated operation. Compare every demo for changed and preserved coordinates, including clusters that look similar but receive different treatment.

After a wrong submission, reject the complete hypothesis and revisit the transformation family; don't make cosmetic coordinate edits. Separate trigger, propagation geometry, selected extent, and handling of original markers. A single demo that extends a diagonal may support a ray hypothesis, but unchanged sparse clusters elsewhere are counterevidence to blindly extending every similar-looking track. Request more only when an example can distinguish specific alternatives; otherwise form a materially revised candidate from the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stripe-sequence-validation.md", "content": "# Uniform stripe sequence tasks: validation and revision

For grids made of full-width, single-color rows, collapse each grid to a 1-D sequence of row colors before inferring the transformation. Compare input and output sequences position by position, including leading and trailing background rows and every gap between visible stripes. Check whether output preserves anchor rows, repeats a motif, extends a sequence, or uses a phase determined by the first/last marker. Do not infer a 2-D periodic tiling merely because other tasks in the broader benchmark use diagonal traces; select the transformation family supported by the current demos.

After a wrong answer, binary feedback rejects the whole candidate but gives no localization. Re-evaluate the rule itself against all demos rather than making repeated near-duplicate grids. Before serializing, validate the output dimensions, count every row's width, and ensure all cell values are valid integers 0–9.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/stripe-sequence-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/rejected-submission-revision.md", "content": "# Revising after a wrong submission

A wrong answer rejects the whole candidate rule, not just the conspicuous grid cell you suspect. Binary feedback cannot localize the error. Before another action, revisit every demonstration and separate possible failure points: trigger, target extent, output/color mapping, and regions that must remain unchanged. Compare those predictions against the test structure. Do not make a cosmetic local edit to the rejected grid unless the demos independently identify that exact defect.

A new demo is worthwhile only when you can state a concrete competing-rule distinction and explain how its possible outcomes would change the test output. Otherwise, construct a materially revised candidate from current evidence. In all cases preserve the strict one-action JSON protocol.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/rejected-submission-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md", "content": "# Revising hypotheses after incorrect submissions

A wrong grid rejects the entire submitted hypothesis, but binary feedback does not locate the error. Do not respond by shifting the same speculative recoloring boundary or trying arbitrary minor variants. First compare every demo input/output cellwise and list the precise changed coordinates, including isolated endpoint changes and preserved lines. Distinguish object-internal changes from continuation/tail changes, and test predictions against all examples.

Request another demo only when you can state which live candidate rules it could separate. Otherwise derive a materially different rule from the examples and apply it once, preserving all cells not selected by that rule. This discipline prevents a sequence of unsupported guesses from spending the attempt budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/multiple-outline-effects.md", "content": "# Distinguishing multiple outlined-object effects

When a scene contains several hollow or outlined motifs, create a per-object ledger: bounds, border color, interior pattern, embedded exceptional cells, and exact output changes. Infer the trigger independently from the operation and its extent. A task may combine independent effects—for example, filling the interior of one enclosure while changing an exceptional-color center cell in other motifs. Do not assume that every similar-looking enclosure receives the same operation or that all interiors should be filled globally.

Compare each object role across every demo, including unchanged objects and preserved borders. After a rejected output, discard the whole selection/operation hypothesis and audit every object rather than tweaking isolated cells. Request a demo only if competing object-selection rules predict different test outputs and the information justifies its cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/multiple-outline-effects.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/rejected-fill-candidate-review.md", "content": "# Reassessing a rejected broad transformation

A binary incorrect submission rejects the entire candidate rule; it does not localize the error to a particular cell or boundary. Re-derive the rule using the demos rather than making a small cosmetic change to the submitted grid. Explicitly inspect the proposed trigger, transformed extent, and conspicuous regions left untouched by demonstrations. For region-fill hypotheses, distinguish changing the target object's cells from recoloring surrounding background, deleting markers, or modifying unrelated shapes. If these interpretations predict different test outputs, request a demo only when its expected information is worth the fixed cost; otherwise submit a materially revised, evidence-grounded candidate.

During strict evaluator turns, emit exactly one protocol-valid JSON action and no commentary.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/rejected-fill-candidate-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md", "content": "# Rule audit after a rejected submission

A wrong submission rejects the whole hypothesis, not just one guessed cell. Re-derive from all demo pairs rather than iterating local edits. In cluttered inputs, identify regular object geometry and compare each output cell: isolated cells of other colors within otherwise uniform shapes may be noise or overlays, and the output may normalize the whole object while removing those intrusions. Conversely, do not generalize this without demo support. Audit both the transformed extent and every unusual cell outside it; test the candidate against every demo before spending another attempt.

When a submitted grid is not clearly supported, a further demo can be more valuable than another guess, but specify which competing interpretations it could distinguish. Keep strict protocol: exactly one JSON action per puzzle turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md", "content": "# Learning panel-label rules from compressed outputs

When a separator-delimited grid shrinks to one output cell per panel, the lattice and ordering are only the first inference step. A plausible grid with matching dimensions can still be wrong because the cell-label semantics are underdetermined.

## Panel evidence ledger

For every demonstration, align each panel with its output coordinate and record:
- marker/background/separator colors;
- all exceptional-cell coordinates, counts, and arrangement;
- whether features align across panel rows or columns;
- the output value for that panel.

Normalize palettes across examples. Test panel-local rules (presence, count, shape, location) and cross-panel rules (aligned feature sequences, repeated rows/columns, or spatial configurations) against every cell of every output. Distinguish selecting a panel from assigning its output label; the output can encode a relationship rather than copy the local marker color.

## Costly action checkpoint

After each demo, update the ledger before requesting another. Name the competing rules and whether they produce different predictions on the active test. After an incorrect submission, preserve only validated structural facts (such as panel dimensions/order), discard the rejected semantic mapping, and rebuild from all demonstrations. Binary feedback identifies no particular cell, so do not make arbitrary local edits. Spend another demo only when it can discriminate live rules enough to justify its fixed cost.

Always preserve the strict one-action JSON protocol when the evaluator requires it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/instance-learning-panel-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/repeated-failure-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Repeated failures in costly ARC instances

When several submissions remain incorrect after many demonstrations, stop making loosely motivated cell-level revisions. Binary feedback rejects the entire rule and gives no localization. Reconstruct the full evidence ledger: exact changed and preserved cells, object and panel boundaries, exceptions, and each candidate's predictions across every demo. Then select a materially different structural hypothesis (for example, an aligned panel relation, localized overlay, or directional propagation) only if it explains the demonstrations better. Validate the whole proposed grid against all examples before submission. If demos are exhausted, avoid repeating prior grids and choose the strongest evidence-grounded alternative.

This complements the general checkpoint: demos must be reconciled immediately, and every action remains exactly one protocol-valid JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/repeated-failure-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/consecutive-demo-requests.md", "content": "# Post-demo checkpoint before spending again

After every demo arrives, compare its changed and unchanged cells against all earlier pairs before choosing the next action. Record the exact changed pattern, the candidate rule, preserved counterexamples, and a specific uncertainty a further example could discriminate. If no such uncertainty remains articulate, do not make another generic demo request; submit the best-supported rule. A request sequence without interim reconciliation burns budget without exploiting information already obtained. Keep the evaluator's one-action-per-turn protocol intact.
