{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/panel-exception-lesson.md", "content": "# Panel classification: exceptions and post-rejection audit

In separator-panel tasks, correct panel dimensions do not validate a marker-presence label rule. Across every demonstration, make a full table of panels and outputs, recording marker presence, count, and arrangement. A marked panel may share the background output label while another marked panel receives the special label; train the signature against all examples rather than extrapolating from early uniformly labeled demos.

For a rejected output, treat the whole mapping as suspect. Recheck panel extraction and reading order, then revisit what feature distinguishes labels. Do not merely edit a few cells based on binary feedback. Request another example only if surviving signatures predict different useful outcomes and the cost is justified; otherwise apply the strongest candidate consistently to all test panels.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/panel-exception-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/interior-fill-and-outline-audit.md", "content": "# Interior fills versus outline preservation

When demos contain outlined objects alongside filled rectangles, avoid conflating their transformations. Compare input/output cellwise and classify each changed coordinate as object interior, object boundary, adjacent panel, or unrelated background. In observed ARC patterns, enclosed background cells can become a fill color while the original colored outline remains unchanged; the same task may independently fill the interior of a different framed rectangle. Validate each proposed target region against every demo and preserve the outline unless changed cells establish otherwise.

After an incorrect submission, re-evaluate both the selection rule and target extent. Binary rejection does not identify whether the mistake was the target objects, fill color, boundary handling, or omitted regions. Map all changes before constructing the test output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/interior-fill-and-outline-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-submit-boundary-review.md", "content": "# Revising after a failed region-fill submission

A demo showed a long stripe of a distinct marker color continuing into a rightward tail, with the tail cells recolored to a third color. The test contained multiple irregular 8-valued shapes on a 6-valued background. An initial interpretation extended the third color across much of the presumed bounding region, including holes/interior background gaps and detached regions, and was rejected. The lesson is to avoid turning a directional continuation observation into a blanket region fill: identify the precise motif/stripe that triggers propagation, its direction and endpoint, and distinguish those tail cells from all other background cells. Binary rejection cannot locate the mistaken assumption; revise the entire selection rule against demo boundaries before resubmitting. For ambiguity between region-fill and stripe-tail hypotheses, spend a demo only if it can discriminate them.
